Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Gothic period live-action; candlelight, deep shadows, 1840s dress. One medium throughout.

FADE IN:

INT. CANDLELIT ROOM - NIGHT

A bare, close room. One candle. NARRATOR, a lean man in his thirties, pale, dark hollowed eyes, neat but threadbare waistcoat, sits forward on a plain wooden chair and looks straight at us—calm, almost smiling.

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

He steadies his hands on his knees.

NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

A shuttered, spare bedchamber. Heavy bed. Dark wood. OLD MAN, frail, white-haired, sits by the window in a worn dressing-gown. One pale blue eye is filmed over—vulture-like. NARRATOR (younger coat, same man) stands near, attentive, almost tender, pouring tea.

The filmed eye turns toward him. NARRATOR's face tightens; his blood runs cold in the look alone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. CANDLELIT ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR leans in, sharp, proud.

NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work! I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Midnight. Thick darkness. Shutters fastened. NARRATOR eases the latch and opens the door a hair's breadth—oh, so gently. He slides in a dark lantern, all closed. Then his head. Slow. Very, very slow.

An hour of stillness. His whole head is inside. He undoes the lantern a crack; hinges threaten to creak. A single thin ray seeks the pillow.

The OLD MAN sleeps. The eye is closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

Morning light through a slit. NARRATOR strides in boldly, hearty, kind.

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night?

OLD MAN
Well enough. Well enough.

NARRATOR smiles, helps him rise—never kinder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The eighth night. NARRATOR at the door, more than usually cautious. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than his hand on the latch. He pushes steadily, steadily. Triumph tightens his mouth; he almost chuckles.

On the bed, OLD MAN stirs, startled.

NARRATOR freezes, head in the opening, about to open the lantern. His thumb slips on the tin fastening.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

Silence. NARRATOR does not move a muscle. For a whole hour he holds. OLD MAN sits up in the black, listening—hearkening as to death-watches in the wall.

A slight groan—mortal terror, low, stifled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew the sound well. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. He had been saying to himself—It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor—or It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp. All in vain; because Death, in approaching him had stalked with his black shadow before him.

NARRATOR opens a very little crevice in the lantern. A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, falls full upon the vulture eye.

It is open—wide, wide open. Dull blue. Hideous veil. Nothing else of the face—only the damned spot.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The sound grows—quicker, louder. NARRATOR holds the ray steady, scarcely breathing. The hellish tattoo swells in the dreadful silence of the old house.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps in. OLD MAN shrieks once—once only. NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

NARRATOR smiles gaily. Beneath the bed, for many minutes, the heart beats on—muffled.

A long hold. The muffled beat continues. NARRATOR waits, head tilted, listening.

At length it ceases.

NARRATOR removes the bed. Examines the corpse. Places his hand upon the heart and holds it there many minutes. No pulsation.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, he was stone, stone dead. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. CANDLELIT ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR's eyes shine with pride.

NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The night wanes. NARRATOR works hastily, in silence. He dismembers the corpse—head, arms, legs.

He takes up three planks from the flooring and deposits all between the scantlings. Replaces the boards so cleverly that no human eye could detect anything wrong. A tub has caught every stain. No blood-spot whatever.

He stands over the seamless floor, breathing hard, satisfied.

A distant bell sounds four. Still dark as midnight.

A knocking at the street door.

INT. HOUSE ENTRY - NIGHT

NARRATOR opens the door with a light heart. Three men in coats and badges: OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HAYES, OFFICER BRIGGS—suave, correct.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play. We are to search the premises.

NARRATOR smiles—what has he to fear?—and bids them welcome.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR leads them through. Shows the old man's treasures, secure, undisturbed. In the wild audacity of his triumph he brings chairs and seats the officers. He places his own chair upon the very spot beneath which reposes the corpse.

The officers sit, satisfied. His manner has convinced them. They rest from their fatigues and chat of familiar things. NARRATOR answers cheerily.

Ere long he pales. He wishes them gone. A ringing in his ears. He talks more freely to rid himself of it—but the noise gains definiteness. It is not within his ears.

A low, dull, quick sound—such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR gasps. The officers hear it not. He talks more quickly, more vehemently. The noise steadily increases.

He rises, argues about trifles in a high key, violent gesticulations. Paces the floor with heavy strides. Foams. Raves. Swears. Swings his chair and grates it upon the boards—but the noise arises over all.

Louder—louder—louder. The men chat on pleasantly and smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror! Anything was better than this agony! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die! and now—again!—hark! louder! louder! louder! louder!

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

He claws at the floorboards. The officers spring up.

INT. CANDLELIT ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR sits as at the first—candle low, face drained, still looking at us. The calm is gone. Only the nerves remain.

He listens to something we cannot quite hear.

FADE OUT.

THE END
